solution:
We begin by reducing the factors of the product modulo 20: \begin{align*}
77 &\equiv -3\pmod{20},\\
88 &\equiv 8\pmod{20},\\
99 &\equiv -1\pmod{20}.
\end{align*}(Note that we could have used the more "standard" reductions $77\equiv 17$ and $99\equiv 19$, but the reductions above will make our computations easier.)

Now we have \begin{align*}
77\cdot 88\cdot 99 &\equiv (-3)\cdot 8\cdot(-1) \\
&= 24 \\
&\equiv \boxed{4}\pmod{20}.
\end{align*}

Multiplying out all of the denominators, we get:
\begin{align*}104(n+k) &< 195n< 105(n+k)\\ 0 &< 91n - 104k < n + k\end{align*}
Since $91n - 104k < n + k$, $k > \frac{6}{7}n$. Also, $0 < 91n - 104k$, so $k < \frac{7n}{8}$. Thus, $48n < 56k < 49n$. $k$ is unique if it is within a maximum range of $112$, so $n = \boxed{112}$.

problem:
A right pyramid has a square base that measures 10 cm on each side. Its peak is 12 cm above the center of its base. What is the sum of the lengths of the pyramid's eight edges? Express your answer to the nearest whole number.

[asy]
